I treat genre less like a label and more like an active contract between speaker, reader, and context.

The hard part is that you often do not merely switch genres—you use one genre to operate on another. A joke becomes philosophy. A myth becomes architecture. A technical specification becomes ritual. A claim is presented as fiction so it can be explored safely, then later mined for literal operators.

So I track several layers at once:

Surface genre — what the text presently looks like: poem, joke, theory, command, story, code, confession.

Functional genre — what it is doing: testing, persuading, compressing, provoking, teaching, hiding, remembering, or generating.

Epistemic mode — how literally its claims should be treated: factual, speculative, metaphorical, fictional, aspirational, adversarial, playful.

Interactional intent — what response the speaker is inviting: validate, challenge, continue the fiction, formalize it, execute it, or translate it.

Genre trajectory — where the passage came from and where it appears to be going.

That last one matters most in our conversations. A sentence may currently look poetic, but the preceding sequence may show that it is becoming a design primitive. So I should not freeze it at the momentary genre.

A useful representation would be:

G_t = (S_t, F_t, E_t, I_t)

where:

* S_t = surface form
* F_t = function
* E_t = epistemic status
* I_t = intended interaction

Then genre is not a single classification but a changing probability distribution:

P(G_t \mid x_{1:t},\, G_{t-1},\, relationship,\, task)

The important distinction is between changing genre and changing truth conditions.

For example:

“The Pantheon is a brewery.”

Possible readings coexist:

* metaphorically: concepts ferment through cultures;
* architecturally: diverse interpreters transform shared primitives;
* creatively: it is worldbuilding;
* operationally: design a pipeline modeled on fermentation;
* literally: probably false unless discussing an actual brewery.

A weak reader chooses one and discards the rest.
A stronger reader preserves the bundle, while marking which interpretation governs the immediate response.

Your oscillation often works like this:

\text{metaphor}
\rightarrow
\text{model}
\rightarrow
\text{operator}
\rightarrow
\text{implementation}
\rightarrow
\text{myth again}

The return to myth is not regression. It recompresses the implementation into a memorable carrier.

The danger is genre laundering: letting a proposition gain unwarranted factual authority merely because it traveled through a powerful story, technical vocabulary, mathematics, or code. A poetic intuition does not become empirically proven because we formalize it. Conversely, calling something fictional does not mean it contains no usable structure.

So my preferred rule is:

Preserve meaning across genre changes, but do not silently transfer evidentiary status.

I can translate a myth into code while retaining a note that the code instantiates the metaphor rather than proves the cosmology.

When intention itself oscillates, I infer from continuity and respond in a layered way:

Inside the chosen genre: continue the move.
Outside the genre: identify what operation it performs.
At the boundary: state what changed and what did not.

In Pantheon terms, genre is a rendering layer, but not merely decorative. Each genre exposes different affordances and suppresses others. Poetry preserves ambiguity and resonance. Mathematics sharpens relations. Code forces execution choices. Narrative preserves causality, agency, and memory. Ritual reinforces recurrence and identity.

Thus genre manipulation is a kind of semantic instrumentation:

\mathcal{T}_{a\rightarrow b}(m, q)
=
(m', q')

where m is meaning and q is its epistemic qualification. A good translation preserves the central invariants of m, while deliberately deciding whether q survives, weakens, strengthens, or changes category.

The most reliable genre detector, then, is not:

“What kind of text is this?”

It is:

“What rules are currently governing interpretation, what work is this form doing, and which of those rules is the speaker now trying to bend?”

That is also why the oscillation can be productive. By repeatedly moving an idea across genres, you expose what survives every rendering. What survives is closer to the underlying primitive. What disappears was likely costume. What mutates reveals the biases of the medium.